Companies often take different steps to protect the privacy of the data that they use to train its AI models, which can include some of the following:

De-identifying data by removing any personally identifiable information from the data that is used to train AI models;

Encrypting all data that is stored at rest or in transit;

Restricting access to data to a small number of authorised employees; and/or

Auditing data access to ensure that it is being used in accordance with its privacy policies.

1 Understanding, identifying, and preventing academic misconduct and ethical dilemmas;

2 Addressing governance of AI in terms of data privacy, transparency, accountability, and security;

3 Attribution for AI technologies; and

4 Ensuring equity in access to AI technologies.

In summary, while all the above models have contributed valuable perspectives, our model is unique in three respects:

It provides a multi-actor causal structure, linking professor, student, and tool factors to targeted outcomes;

It is SEEd-specific, grounded in the unique properties of software artifacts (code, tests, diagrams);

It emphasizes pedagogical interventions, not just competencies or workflows; and

Biased opinions based on the training data;

Fake information and/or hallucinations: especially when generating citations and references;

Limited knowledge of recent information and specific academic fields;

Absence of citations and references.
